WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:06.753
Meghan Sullivan: It strikes me,
as somebody who's a huge space --

00:00:06.795 --> 00:00:07.921
Ariel Ekblaw: Nerd.

00:00:07.963 --> 00:00:09.548
MS: Fangirl is what I would say,

00:00:09.548 --> 00:00:13.051
is that having these really concrete
and ambitious goals,

00:00:13.051 --> 00:00:16.179
like going to the moon
or putting people on the space station,

00:00:16.221 --> 00:00:19.140
sometimes forces us to focus
our attention on solving problems

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.893
that then help us solve problems
back here on earth.

00:00:21.935 --> 00:00:24.229
AE: This is exactly
what we want to do in space.

00:00:25.564 --> 00:00:28.567
[Intersections]

00:00:34.781 --> 00:00:37.742
[Ariel Ekblaw, Space architect]

00:00:38.368 --> 00:00:41.329
[Meghan Sullivan, Philosopher]

00:00:42.497 --> 00:00:43.748
AE: Off we go.

00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:47.877
What does the common good actually mean

00:00:47.919 --> 00:00:49.796
and how do we build towards it?

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:54.050
MS: I think a lot of people
assume that the common good means

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:57.387
finding the kind of least common
denominator that we can agree on,

00:00:57.429 --> 00:00:59.139
like just splitting the difference

00:00:59.139 --> 00:01:02.183
or kind of, finding like,
going down to the basics.

00:01:02.225 --> 00:01:04.227
I think that is a huge mistake.

00:01:04.853 --> 00:01:09.733
The philosophers that I study and love,
the ancient Greeks, like Aristotle,

00:01:09.774 --> 00:01:11.943
believe that the common good,
the good itself,

00:01:11.943 --> 00:01:13.862
is something that you're moving towards.

00:01:13.903 --> 00:01:17.073
It's a common goal
and nobody is there yet.

00:01:17.616 --> 00:01:20.660
So when we talk about prioritizing
the common good,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.747
I think we're not trying to figure out,

00:01:23.747 --> 00:01:26.458
of all the things that we believe
and care about now,

00:01:26.499 --> 00:01:27.792
how do we sort of settle --

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:29.794
AE: And figure out what we already share.

00:01:29.836 --> 00:01:31.004
MS: Exactly.

00:01:31.046 --> 00:01:33.256
Instead, we need to be
having this conversation

00:01:33.256 --> 00:01:35.342
of where do we collectively
want to move to,

00:01:35.342 --> 00:01:38.303
which presumably is very different
from where we currently are,

00:01:38.345 --> 00:01:39.971
which is probably not that great.

00:01:40.305 --> 00:01:42.140
AE: I think this is really compelling.

00:01:42.182 --> 00:01:44.809
In our field for space,
we think about the space commons

00:01:44.851 --> 00:01:46.811
and try to define what is the public good

00:01:46.853 --> 00:01:50.774
in the context of a really
broad geographical expanse,

00:01:50.774 --> 00:01:53.068
like the near-Earth cocoon of space.

00:01:53.068 --> 00:01:55.070
And we don't have
a real space commons yet.

00:01:55.111 --> 00:01:56.363
It does exist as a space,

00:01:56.363 --> 00:01:58.323
but we haven't defined all the norms yet.

00:01:58.365 --> 00:01:59.532
And so I love this,

00:01:59.574 --> 00:02:02.077
this is a way that we can think
about moving together

00:02:02.118 --> 00:02:04.287
towards the definition
of maybe a future goal.

00:02:04.287 --> 00:02:07.415
An aspirational version
of humanity's commons.

00:02:07.457 --> 00:02:09.125
MS: And maybe trying to figure out

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:12.295
what it means to be
an interplanetary species.

00:02:12.295 --> 00:02:15.590
We might imagine, like,
exercise our imagination

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:19.052
about how to be a better
planetary species in the process.

00:02:19.094 --> 00:02:20.595
AE: Love that.

00:02:22.889 --> 00:02:24.099
MS: Alright.

00:02:25.684 --> 00:02:28.770
What do we owe each other as humans?

00:02:28.812 --> 00:02:30.271
AE: Hmm.

00:02:30.313 --> 00:02:35.610
I think we should owe each other
the alternative to the Hobbesian view.

00:02:35.652 --> 00:02:40.657
The idea that human nature
is fundamentally wrought at its base,

00:02:40.657 --> 00:02:44.536
or somehow pegged
to our lowest basis instinct.

00:02:44.536 --> 00:02:48.331
I think we should owe each other
our highest version of ourselves

00:02:48.373 --> 00:02:51.376
or our more honorable
version of ourselves.

00:02:51.376 --> 00:02:52.585
MS: Oh, I like that.

00:02:52.585 --> 00:02:54.003
So you disagree with Hobbes

00:02:54.003 --> 00:02:57.424
that life should be nasty,
brutish, and short.

00:02:57.465 --> 00:02:59.092
That's pretty, pretty depressing.

00:02:59.092 --> 00:03:01.928
AE: It should be elevated,
you know, delightful and long.

00:03:01.928 --> 00:03:04.723
MS: One of my favorite
20th-century philosophers

00:03:04.764 --> 00:03:07.142
is [a] philosopher named Iris Murdoch.

00:03:07.183 --> 00:03:09.269
And she has a good answer
to this question.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:13.773
She says, what we owe another person
is to be willing to look at them again.

00:03:14.315 --> 00:03:16.818
Which means like,
oftentimes we meet people,

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.446
and we form our assumptions
and stereotypes about them.

00:03:19.487 --> 00:03:21.656
We put them in a little
category in our brain,

00:03:21.656 --> 00:03:24.367
and then we interact with them
according to that category.

00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:26.161
AE: We put them in a box too early.

00:03:26.161 --> 00:03:27.328
MS: Oh my gosh.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.247
And she's like, 80 percent of the time,

00:03:29.289 --> 00:03:31.082
your first impression is pretty dumb.

00:03:31.082 --> 00:03:33.334
AE: This is something
I have learned in my life.

00:03:33.376 --> 00:03:34.878
I think for the last 10 years,

00:03:34.878 --> 00:03:36.921
I thought I was great at first impressions

00:03:36.921 --> 00:03:39.257
and then judging people
in a positive sense,

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:40.592
but quickly and decisively.

00:03:40.633 --> 00:03:42.343
And I think I have learned recently

00:03:42.343 --> 00:03:44.763
that I really do need
to give people a second look.

00:03:44.763 --> 00:03:45.930
I love this comment.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.934
MS: And just being able to like,
say, alright, I've got a vibe on you.

00:03:49.934 --> 00:03:52.771
I'm going to just put this
on the shelf and just take it --

00:03:52.812 --> 00:03:54.814
And she's like, really look at the person.

00:03:54.856 --> 00:03:57.317
Just make yourself see
like, there's a person there.

00:03:57.317 --> 00:04:00.403
So I think if we did that,
maybe things would start to go better.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:03.698
AE: That was a great toss.

00:04:05.366 --> 00:04:08.953
If you could design a better world
for humans from scratch,

00:04:08.995 --> 00:04:10.538
what's the first rule?

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:15.293
MS: You know, I come at philosophy
from a Catholic perspective.

00:04:15.335 --> 00:04:18.838
And in the Jewish tradition
and the Christian tradition,

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.383
there was a fundamental rule
that was laid down at the beginning

00:04:22.383 --> 00:04:24.803
that then we just promptly disregarded,

00:04:24.844 --> 00:04:30.683
which is you will love your neighbor
as another self, like, as yourself,

00:04:30.725 --> 00:04:32.185
like, you're going to put love

00:04:32.185 --> 00:04:35.271
at the basis of all the rest
of your decision making.

00:04:35.271 --> 00:04:36.898
And pretty much as soon

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.318
as that kind of fundamental
operating principle was laid down,

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.404
we all started ignoring it
and like, revising it.

00:04:43.863 --> 00:04:48.076
So I don't know if I would
revise the original code,

00:04:48.117 --> 00:04:51.579
but I would maybe
enforce it a little bit harder.

00:04:51.579 --> 00:04:53.665
I don't know if that's
allowed as an answer.

00:04:53.665 --> 00:04:54.874
What would you do?

00:04:54.916 --> 00:04:57.210
AE: I think in the context
of space environments,

00:04:57.252 --> 00:04:59.546
we're often in isolated,
confined environments,

00:04:59.587 --> 00:05:02.006
tiny, constrained little spaces.

00:05:02.048 --> 00:05:04.717
So if I was going to start
with a new rule for humanity,

00:05:04.717 --> 00:05:06.594
I love the idea of an oldie but goodie,

00:05:06.636 --> 00:05:09.889
the golden rule: treat others
how you would like to be treated.

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:12.475
And I think that that actually
would play out very well

00:05:12.517 --> 00:05:15.895
in a constrained space
where what goes around comes around,

00:05:15.895 --> 00:05:17.522
all happens very quickly,

00:05:17.564 --> 00:05:21.276
with kind of a small, you know,
proto group of humans in a space habitat.

00:05:21.276 --> 00:05:24.153
It's like almost a tiny little,
maybe not a civilization,

00:05:24.195 --> 00:05:26.656
but a tiny little society
in its own right.

00:05:26.656 --> 00:05:29.909
So yeah, something like
recommitting to the golden rule

00:05:29.951 --> 00:05:32.120
and this sense of really
treating other people

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.498
in the way in which you would like
to be received and seen and treated.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:36.666
MS: I love it.

00:05:38.418 --> 00:05:39.878
You're going to love this one.

00:05:39.919 --> 00:05:44.549
What is a hope you have for humans
in the next ten years?

00:05:45.425 --> 00:05:47.427
AE: I have an aspiration for humans:

00:05:47.468 --> 00:05:51.347
that more people get to experience
the overview effect,

00:05:51.347 --> 00:05:53.892
which really means
democratizing access to space

00:05:53.892 --> 00:05:56.728
so that more humans can fly up
to low-Earth orbit,

00:05:56.769 --> 00:05:59.731
look back down at the Earth
and see the Earth from space.

00:05:59.772 --> 00:06:03.067
And even really
serious military astronauts,

00:06:03.109 --> 00:06:07.447
very, you know, impeccably
well-trained, brave people,

00:06:07.488 --> 00:06:10.491
they start to cry when they see
the Earth from space.

00:06:10.533 --> 00:06:13.828
They lose control over their own
emotions because it is so moving.

00:06:13.828 --> 00:06:17.081
And I would love to share
that experience with more people.

00:06:17.123 --> 00:06:18.416
That's a hope I would have.

00:06:18.458 --> 00:06:21.419
MS: I think, on my end,
similar hope --

00:06:21.419 --> 00:06:23.212
that we’d have a kind of realization,

00:06:23.212 --> 00:06:28.176
I'm very excited about the AI
decade finally taking root.

00:06:28.176 --> 00:06:32.805
And I consider myself kind of pragmatic
optimist about artificial intelligence.

00:06:32.805 --> 00:06:34.599
But my biggest hope

00:06:34.641 --> 00:06:38.227
is that we would use
this powerful technology

00:06:38.269 --> 00:06:40.563
that we now have at our disposal

00:06:40.605 --> 00:06:45.443
to help a whole lot more folks realize
what it means to be genuinely human,

00:06:45.485 --> 00:06:47.403
that like, kind of humans be the star.

00:06:48.071 --> 00:06:49.447
It heightens the difference,

00:06:49.447 --> 00:06:51.699
and also, there's so much
about our lives pre-AI

00:06:51.741 --> 00:06:53.076
that [is] dehumanizing,

00:06:53.117 --> 00:06:56.162
so many ways in which
we learn, in which we work,

00:06:56.162 --> 00:06:57.538
that have never enabled us

00:06:57.538 --> 00:07:01.417
to really express what's special
about being a human being.

00:07:01.459 --> 00:07:03.878
I'd love to think that now
we have this technology,

00:07:03.878 --> 00:07:06.798
we would choose to develop it
in a way where humans,

00:07:06.839 --> 00:07:09.926
and our capabilities and virtues
would be the star of the show.

00:07:09.926 --> 00:07:11.886
I don't know that that's going to happen.

00:07:11.928 --> 00:07:13.304
I'm kind of afraid it's not,

00:07:13.346 --> 00:07:14.806
but maybe if we had the chance

00:07:14.806 --> 00:07:17.642
to see where we stand
in the perspective of the universe,

00:07:17.684 --> 00:07:19.185
it would go better.

00:07:21.396 --> 00:07:24.732
AE: What is the biggest barrier
to human thriving?

00:07:24.732 --> 00:07:26.734
MS: The biggest barrier to human thriving?

00:07:26.776 --> 00:07:29.278
There are some really big barriers,

00:07:29.278 --> 00:07:34.325
but I think probably the biggest
one is our inattention.

00:07:35.451 --> 00:07:39.539
You know, we're rational animals,

00:07:39.539 --> 00:07:44.752
we have extraordinarily
great capabilities in us,

00:07:44.752 --> 00:07:48.423
but the only way that we realize them,
unlike most animals,

00:07:48.423 --> 00:07:51.342
which kind of mature and develop
their powers no matter what,

00:07:51.342 --> 00:07:56.014
a squirrel becomes a good squirrel
with very little effort.

00:07:56.055 --> 00:07:57.849
Humans require a great deal of effort

00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:00.393
to become good versions
of what we're supposed to be,

00:08:00.393 --> 00:08:03.354
and [that] requires a lot of attention,
not just to ourselves,

00:08:03.354 --> 00:08:05.898
but attention on the part
of our parents and teachers.

00:08:05.898 --> 00:08:09.652
And I think when we fail to flourish,

00:08:09.652 --> 00:08:14.991
it's not typically because
we've had some malicious plan

00:08:15.033 --> 00:08:16.784
that's screwed up our lives.

00:08:16.784 --> 00:08:19.287
But really, we just never
had the opportunity

00:08:19.328 --> 00:08:20.955
to focus on the good plan.

00:08:20.955 --> 00:08:22.957
So I would think distraction.

00:08:22.957 --> 00:08:24.208
How about you?

00:08:24.208 --> 00:08:25.585
AE: That's a great response.

00:08:25.585 --> 00:08:28.421
Like, we are really
high-intensity creatures

00:08:28.463 --> 00:08:30.423
in the sense we need a lot of attention.

00:08:30.423 --> 00:08:32.592
We need a lot of explicit
care and development

00:08:32.592 --> 00:08:34.844
to become maybe the best
version of ourselves.

00:08:34.844 --> 00:08:36.220
For me, it's interesting.

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:38.264
I think of this sometimes in the context

00:08:38.264 --> 00:08:41.350
of why do we spend so much money
on space exploration

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.352
if some of the barriers to human thriving

00:08:43.352 --> 00:08:45.480
are things like poverty,

00:08:45.521 --> 00:08:47.065
war, climate change,

00:08:47.106 --> 00:08:49.150
there's a lot of things
at a societal scale

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:52.528
that might be keeping us from thriving
in the way that we really could.

00:08:52.528 --> 00:08:54.238
And then there arises the question,

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.991
why would we spend money
on space at all in that context.

00:08:56.991 --> 00:09:00.036
And I think one of the things
we could do to help human thriving

00:09:00.078 --> 00:09:02.789
is we can use space
for the benefit of life on Earth.

00:09:02.789 --> 00:09:06.709
So before we go [and] be
a sci-fi spacefaring species,

00:09:06.751 --> 00:09:09.670
maybe we earn our right
to go do that someday

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:11.756
by showing that we can
take really good care

00:09:11.756 --> 00:09:13.341
of our own planet first.

00:09:13.341 --> 00:09:16.803
So try to do things in space
that would help with human thriving.

00:09:16.803 --> 00:09:18.930
And my latest favorite one is biology.

00:09:18.971 --> 00:09:22.266
It turns out biology is really different
when you're floating in orbit

00:09:22.266 --> 00:09:23.810
than when you're on the ground.

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:26.687
And so we might be able
to do things like cancer cures,

00:09:26.729 --> 00:09:28.606
Alzheimer's, organoids, testing,

00:09:28.648 --> 00:09:31.984
all kinds of interesting things
that would maybe help humans thrive

00:09:31.984 --> 00:09:34.612
in a more concrete,
immediate way on Earth,

00:09:34.654 --> 00:09:37.115
but leveraging space technology.

00:09:37.156 --> 00:09:39.659
MS: It strikes me as somebody
who's a huge space --

00:09:39.659 --> 00:09:40.827
AE: Nerd?

00:09:40.827 --> 00:09:42.620
MS: Fangirl is what I would say,

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:46.541
is that having these really concrete
and ambitious goals,

00:09:46.582 --> 00:09:49.669
like going to the moon
or putting people on the space station

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:51.671
sometimes forces us
to focus our attention

00:09:51.671 --> 00:09:55.174
on solving problems that then help us
solve problems back here on earth.

00:09:55.174 --> 00:09:57.468
And having a big vision like that,

00:09:57.468 --> 00:10:00.012
sometimes [is] exactly
the kick in the pants you need

00:10:00.012 --> 00:10:03.141
to feel motivated to take on something
that in your ordinary life

00:10:03.141 --> 00:10:05.351
might not feel like
it deserves your attention.

00:10:05.351 --> 00:10:07.603
AE: This is exactly
what we want to do in space.

00:10:08.521 --> 00:10:09.814
Producer: Nice.

00:10:10.982 --> 00:10:15.611
MS: How do we build systems
that influence the good in each other?

00:10:15.611 --> 00:10:17.947
AE: Systems that influence
the good in each other

00:10:17.989 --> 00:10:21.242
also have to assume
good intentions at the base.

00:10:21.284 --> 00:10:24.579
And so being able to think
about what is the architecture of policy

00:10:24.579 --> 00:10:26.622
that comes out of the US government,

00:10:26.664 --> 00:10:29.250
or ways that we could unify people
to get them there.

00:10:29.292 --> 00:10:31.294
And I've recently become really passionate

00:10:31.294 --> 00:10:33.546
about something completely
outside of my domain,

00:10:33.588 --> 00:10:35.298
so maybe I'm a little over my skis,

00:10:35.339 --> 00:10:39.719
which is an idea of a national service,
not necessarily a military draft.

00:10:39.719 --> 00:10:43.055
My parents are both ex-military --
I’m very supportive of the military,

00:10:43.055 --> 00:10:44.640
but something that would be like

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.060
every young person does something
for a year when they're 18.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:51.147
And it really unites
red and blue-state kids

00:10:51.147 --> 00:10:52.732
and urban and rural kids.

00:10:52.732 --> 00:10:54.483
And it could be Teach for America --

00:10:54.483 --> 00:10:56.652
it could be something
that's service-oriented.

00:10:56.652 --> 00:10:58.529
Also, maybe, military service-oriented,

00:10:58.529 --> 00:11:00.072
but something like that I think.

00:11:00.072 --> 00:11:03.242
Once you get to know the people
of your community and your village

00:11:03.242 --> 00:11:06.037
and your society and your civilization
a little bit better,

00:11:06.037 --> 00:11:08.831
I think you can do a better job
of finding the good in them

00:11:08.873 --> 00:11:11.375
and then maybe building
good systems around them.

00:11:11.417 --> 00:11:12.585
MS: I love that.

00:11:13.419 --> 00:11:16.797
I think on my end,
there are a few institutions

00:11:16.839 --> 00:11:18.466
that really roll up their sleeves

00:11:18.466 --> 00:11:21.928
and do this work
of forming people's characters.

00:11:21.928 --> 00:11:23.679
One is education,

00:11:23.721 --> 00:11:27.475
another would be, like,
church and religious organizations.

00:11:27.516 --> 00:11:28.893
And a third is the media.

00:11:28.935 --> 00:11:32.063
And everybody likes to like,
talk about how awful, you know,

00:11:32.063 --> 00:11:34.148
whatever, fill in the blank
social media is,

00:11:34.148 --> 00:11:37.818
but, you know, genuinely print media,

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:41.572
the news and various sources
still very much form people’s outlook

00:11:41.572 --> 00:11:42.740
and what they hope for

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:45.201
and what they view
as their sort of purpose in life.

00:11:45.243 --> 00:11:51.165
And I think we have sorely
neglected those institutions,

00:11:51.165 --> 00:11:54.043
and a lot of people are really
on the brink of nihilism

00:11:54.085 --> 00:11:55.294
... right now

00:11:55.294 --> 00:11:58.589
about whether we could still
imagine amazing schools,

00:11:58.631 --> 00:12:01.676
whether we could still imagine
on the weekends,

00:12:01.676 --> 00:12:03.469
you going and being with other humans

00:12:03.511 --> 00:12:06.013
and doing some kind
of collective activity.

00:12:06.055 --> 00:12:09.183
And I think if we really
want to design systems

00:12:09.183 --> 00:12:11.060
that will bring out the good in people,

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:13.896
we've got to recognize
that we are social animals

00:12:13.938 --> 00:12:17.483
and that these human-level
institutions need to remain strong

00:12:17.525 --> 00:12:20.069
and need our investment and our attending.

00:12:20.069 --> 00:12:22.405
So I would love to see just, you know,

00:12:22.405 --> 00:12:24.824
a renaissance of hope
in what we're able to build

00:12:24.865 --> 00:12:26.367
in those kinds of institutions.

00:12:26.409 --> 00:12:29.620
AE: I love this and just reinvesting
in bringing humans together

00:12:29.662 --> 00:12:31.414
through these different institutions

00:12:31.414 --> 00:12:33.958
in the ways that we used
to come together and convene

00:12:33.958 --> 00:12:36.168
and gather more,
religious and non-religious.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:38.546
I would love to do that
with you, let's start it.

00:12:38.587 --> 00:12:39.922
MS: Let's do it.

00:12:40.715 --> 00:12:41.841
AE: Thank you so much.

00:12:41.882 --> 00:12:45.011
It was a delight talking to you
and learning more about your work.

00:12:45.011 --> 00:12:46.512
MS: I had so much fun.

